","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-hard-light-of-day-rod-moss"}],"publicationYear":2005,"pageCount":297,"summary":"\"Two years after artist Rod Moss arrived in Alice Springs in Australia's outback to teach painting, he met an indigenous couple who had set up camp in the gully beside his home. Over the next twenty-five years, his friendship with Xavier and Petrina Neil and the friendships that grew from it with the families of Whitegate, an Arrernte aboriginal camp on the outskirts of town, would nourish and challenge Moss beyond his imagining. The Hard Light of Day offers a rare insight into the reality of life in the Outback, from the contours of the MacDonnell Ranges and the textures and sounds of Arrernte culture, to the endemic violence, alcoholism and ill-health that continue to devastate Aboriginal lives.","experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[{"name":"Xavier Neil","confidence":0.9},{"name":"Petrina Neil","confidence":0.9}],"centralPlaces":[{"name":"Alice Springs","confidence":0.98},{"name":"Whitegate","confidence":0.88}],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"Life in Australia's Outback is shaped by Arrernte culture but devastated by endemic violence, alcoholism, and ill-health.","confidence":0.91},"academicOrientation":{"score":32,"confidence":0.85},"profileConfidence":0.96,"lowerConfidenceCandidates":{"centralPlaces":[{"name":"MacDonnell Ranges","confidence":0.72}]},"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"71d16d395d828ff9b3627927ed9347ef16c31243990f26fcc43a2a09fb0cbdaa","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"displaySummary":"The Hard Light of Day offers a rare insight into the reality of life in the Outback, from the contours of the MacDonnell Ranges and the textures and sounds of Arrernte culture, to the endemic violence, alcoholism and ill-health that continue to devastate Aboriginal lives.","primarySubject":"Arts & Criticism","primaryTopic":"Indigenous History","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Arts & Critic